## TL;DR  

**You don’t have to run a tile server or convert the archive.**  
MapLibre iOS can read a remote `.pmtiles` file directly – you just need a small helper library (or a few dozen lines of code) that implements the `pmtiles://` protocol and hands the decoded tiles to MapLibre.  

The easiest way is to add the **official `pmtiles‑swift` package** to your Xcode project, register its URL scheme, and then use a normal `RasterTileSource` / `VectorTileSource` with a `pmtiles://…` template.  
If you prefer not to depend on the package you can write a custom `URLProtocol` (or a thin wrapper around the Rust/Swift PMTiles core) – the idea is the same: intercept the `pmtiles://` URL, fetch the archive (or a cached copy) with HTTP‑range requests, read the tile bytes, and return them to MapLibre.

Below is a step‑by‑step guide, code snippets, and a few practical notes (caching, large‑file handling, offline use, etc.) so you can get the 3 GB basemap working on iOS today.

---

## 1. What “pmtiles://” actually does on the web

On the web you register the protocol with the **npm `pmtiles` package**:

```js
import { register } from 'pmtiles';
register();                     // installs a fetch handler for pmtiles://
```

The handler:

* Issues **HTTP range requests** against the remote `.pmtiles` file.
* Parses the PMTiles index (a tiny SQLite‑like header) to locate the tile.
* Returns the raw tile bytes (vector or raster) to the map library.

MapLibre GL JS then treats the URL exactly like any other tile URL template.

iOS MapLibre has the same architecture – it asks a URL loader for the bytes of a tile URL. The only missing piece is a loader that understands the `pmtiles://` scheme.

---

## 2. The iOS‑native way – use the `pmtiles‑swift` package

### 2.1 Add the package

In Xcode → **File → Add Packages…** and paste:

```
https://github.com/MapLibre/pmtiles-swift
```

Select the latest release (or `main` if you want the newest fixes) and add it to your app target.

### 2.2 Register the scheme (once, e.g. in `AppDelegate`)

```swift
import PMTiles

@main
struct MyApp: App {
    init() {
        // Installs a URLProtocol that knows how to handle pmtiles:// URLs.
        PMTiles.registerScheme()
    }

    var body: some Scene { WindowGroup { ContentView() } }
}
```

> **What this does** – `PMTiles.registerScheme()` installs a custom `URLProtocol` that intercepts any request whose URL scheme is `pmtiles`. The protocol then:
> * Downloads the remote `.pmtiles` file **on‑demand** using HTTP‑range requests (no need to download the whole 3 GB up front).
> * Caches the downloaded chunks in the app’s temporary directory (you can tweak the cache size if you like).
> * Returns the raw tile data to the caller.

### 2.3 Create a source that points at your archive

You can use any of MapLibre’s built‑in source types – the only thing that changes is the URL template.

#### Raster (e.g. PNG/JPEG tiles)

```swift
import MapLibre
import PMTiles

// 1️⃣  The URL template – note the `pmtiles://` prefix.
let rasterTemplate = "pmtiles://https://my-bucket.r2.cloudflarestorage.com/basemap.pmtiles/{z}/{x}/{y}"

// 2️⃣  Create the source.
let rasterSource = RasterTileSource(
    id: "basemap-raster",
    tileURLTemplates: [rasterTemplate],
    tileSize: 512,               // or 256 – whatever your tiles are
    attribution: "© MyData"
)

// 3️⃣  Add it to the style (make sure the style is loaded first).
mapView.style?.addSource(rasterSource)

// 4️⃣  Add a layer that uses the source.
let rasterLayer = RasterLayer(id: "basemap-layer", source: rasterSource)
mapView.style?.addLayer(rasterLayer)
```

#### Vector (e.g. Mapbox‑compatible vector tiles)

```swift
let vectorTemplate = "pmtiles://https://my-bucket.r2.cloudflarestorage.com/basemap.pmtiles/{z}/{x}/{y}"
let vectorSource = VectorTileSource(
    id: "basemap-vector",
    tileURLTemplates: [vectorTemplate],
    attribution: "© MyData"
)

mapView.style?.addSource(vectorSource)

// Example: a line layer that draws from the vector source
let lineLayer = LineLayer(id: "roads", source: vectorSource)
lineLayer.sourceLayer = "road"          // name of the source layer inside the PMTiles
lineLayer.lineColor = .constant(.blue)
mapView.style?.addLayer(lineLayer)
```

That’s it – MapLibre will request tiles like `pmtiles://…/14/8623/5678`, the `PMTiles` URLProtocol will fetch the appropriate byte range from Cloudflare R2, decode the tile, and hand it back to the map.

### 2.4 Optional: Pre‑download / cache the whole archive

If you know the user will be offline for a while, you can download the whole file once and keep it locally:

```swift
let remoteURL = URL(string: "https://my-bucket.r2.cloudflarestorage.com/basemap.pmtiles")!
let localURL = FileManager.default.urls(for: .cachesDirectory, in: .userDomainMask)[0]
                .appendingPathComponent("basemap.pmtiles")

// Simple one‑shot download (you probably want background URLSession + progress UI)
let task = URLSession.shared.downloadTask(with: remoteURL) { tempURL, _, error in
    guard let tempURL = tempURL, error == nil else { return }
    try? FileManager.default.moveItem(at: tempURL, to: localURL)
}
task.resume()
```

After it’s stored locally you can point the source at the file:

```swift
let localTemplate = "pmtiles://\(localURL.path)/{z}/{x}/{y}"
// … same source creation as above
```

The `PMTiles` library automatically detects that the URL points to a local file and reads it directly (no HTTP).

